'''''Ikawaihere''''' is an [[Extinction|extinct]] [[genus]] of [[Megalopidae|megalopid]] [[Actinopterygii|ray-finned fish]], related to modern [[tarpon]], that lived in [[Zealandia]] during the [[Dannevirke epoch]] of the [[Paleogene|Palaeogene]] [[Period (geologic time)|period]]. It contains a single species, '''''I. koehleri''''' from the [[Late Paleocene]] or [[Ypresian|Early Eocene]]-aged [[Red Bluff Tuff Formation]] of [[Pitt Island]] in the [[Chatham Islands]]. The genus name comes from ''ika'', the [[Moriori language|Moriori]] word for fish, and Waihere Bay, the type locality of the specimen. The specific epithet honors its discoverer, Dr. Richard Koehler of the [[University of Otago]].<ref name=":0">{{Cite journal |last=Gottfried |first=Michael D. |last2=Fordyce |first2=R. Ewan |last3=Robinson |first3=Jeffrey H. |last4=Lee |first4=Daphne E. |date=2026 |title=A New Tarpon‐Like Fish (Elopomorpha, Megalopidae) With Exceptional Preservation and Unusual Features From the Paleogene of Pitt Island, Chatham Islands, New Zealand |url=https://rsnz.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1002/jgo2.70022 |journal=New Zealand Journal of Geology and Geophysics |language=en |volume=69 |issue=1 |doi=10.1002/jgo2.70022 |issn=0028-8306}}</ref>

== Discovery ==
The [[type specimen]] represents a three-dimensionally preserved, nearly complete individual about {{Convert|1.2|m|ft}} in length. It was discovered in 1999 eroding out of a cliff {{Convert|40|m|ft}} above sea level by Richard Koehler, and excavation required a tall ladder to be carried across several kilometers of rough terrain to the cliff face. Efforts to describe the fish stalled after the passing of Koehler, which left its locality information uncertain, until Koehler's field notebooks were donated to the University of Otago by his family members.<ref name=":0" /><ref>{{Cite web |last=Otago |first=University of |date=2026-03-03 |title=Study of predatory fossil fish honours late researchers |url=https://www.otago.ac.nz/news/newsroom/study-of-predatory-fossil-fish-honours-late-researchers |access-date=2026-03-03 |website=www.otago.ac.nz |language=en}}</ref>

Although invertebrate fossils from the Red Bluff Tuff Formation have been known since the 1840s, ''Ikawaihere'' is the first vertebrate fossil to be described from the formation. The exceptional preservation of the specimen suggests an extremely rapid burial by [[volcanic tuff]].<ref name=":0" />

== Description ==
''Ikawaihere koehleri'', the [[type species]], is distinguished from other megalopids in possessing an elongate body, a relatively low-profile head, a high and strongly developed [[coronoid process of the mandible]], an enlarged median gular, an extra series of anamestic bones in the cheek region, and the continuation of the lateral line scales as a tapering lobe that extend onto the base of the [[Fish fin|caudal fin]].<ref name=":0" />

